Take the simplest version of this question: a shared counter hit by multiple request-handling threads.

```java
class RequestCounter {
    private int count = 0;

    public void increment() {
        count = count + 1;   // read, add, write — three steps, not one
    }

    public int get() {
        return count;
    }
}
```

`count = count + 1` looks like one operation but is really three: read `count`, add 1, write it back. If two threads interleave between the read and the write, one increment is silently lost — under load this counter undercounts, and the bug never shows up in a single-threaded test.

```java
class RequestCounter {
    private int count = 0;

    public synchronized void increment() {
        count = count + 1;
    }

    public synchronized int get() {
        return count;
    }
}
```

Wrap the read-modify-write in `synchronized` so only one thread executes it at a time. Note `get()` also needs synchronizing — not because reading alone is unsafe, but because without it there is no guarantee the reading thread ever observes the latest write. Lock the smallest region that protects the invariant — here, that's each method body, not the whole class.

```java
class RequestCounter {
    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public void increment() {
        count.incrementAndGet();
    }

    public int get() {
        return count.get();
    }
}
```

For a single counter, `AtomicInteger` is usually the stronger answer than a lock: it uses a hardware compare-and-swap loop instead of blocking, so threads never wait on each other for this one field. Say this out loud if you reach for it — it signals locks aren't your only tool. It stops being the right tool the moment two fields must change together (s09).
